P.S. There is a book called 'innumeracy' by John Allen that describes 
various fallacies that people stumble on in modern world when they are 
figuring out the figures.

One I've stumbled on several time is that people tend to approximate a 
product of two large numbers with the square of their average without 
realising that the square of the average is always larger than the product.

let a = (m+n)/2 then
m = a+p and n = a-p and thus

m*n = a^2-p^2 <= a^2, with equality being valid only when m = n.

